{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לך נא ראה", :translit "lekh na re'eh", :gloss "go now, see"} {:he "בכי טוב", :translit "be-khi tov", :gloss "with a good escort"}], :mechanisms [], :frame {:text "Rashi on Genesis 37:14, citing Bereshit Rabbah 84:13, explains that Jacob told Joseph to go check on his brothers and report back, and that he sent him with a good escort — interpreted as protection or proper companionship for the journey.", :sources ["Rashi on Genesis 37:14" "Bereshit Rabbah 84:13"]}} {:quotes [{:he "והשיבני דבר", :translit "ve-hashiveni davar", :gloss "and bring me back word"} {:he "רוח הקודש", :translit "ruach ha-kodesh", :gloss "the holy spirit"}], :mechanisms [], :frame {:text "The Talmud states that a prophet does not prophesy unless he is in a state of joy, and the term for the divine spirit is ruach ha-kodesh (Shabbat 30b). The concept that speech can unwittingly carry prophetic resonance appears in contexts where biblical figures' words carry unintended future significance (Genesis Rabbah 91:10 on the phrase 'ashurenu,' 'I will see him').", :sources ["Shabbat 30b" "Genesis Rabbah 91:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "עמק", :translit "emek", :gloss "valley / depth"} {:he "רד\"ו", :translit "rad\"v", :gloss "210 (numeric)"} {:he "רד\"ו שנים", :translit "rad\"v shanim", :gloss "210 years"} {:he "וליוהו עד חברון", :translit "ve-levahu ad chevron", :gloss "and he accompanied him to Hebron"} {:he "חזור בך", :translit "chazor bakh", :gloss "turn back / repent"} {:he "ידינו לא שפכה את הדם הזה", :translit "yadeinu lo shafkha et ha-dam ha-zeh", :gloss "our hands did not shed this blood"} {:he "לא פטרנוהו בלא לויה", :translit "lo pitarnuhu belo levayah", :gloss "we did not send him off without escort"} {:he "וירא את העגלות אשר שלח יוסף", :translit "va-yar et ha-agalot asher shalach yosef", :gloss "and he saw the wagons that Joseph sent"} {:he "אל יפטר אדם מחבירו אלא מתוך דברי תורה", :translit "al yipater adam me-chavero ela mi-toch divrei torah", :gloss "a person should not take leave of his fellow except through words of Torah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "עמק", :b "רד\"ו"} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וליוהו עד חברון", :spells "אבא חזור"}], :frame {:text "The Israelites' Egyptian bondage lasted 210 years, a figure derived by counting from the birth of Isaac, which is the tradition recorded by Rashi on Genesis 42:2 citing Seder Olam. The law that elders of a city must declare 'our hands did not shed this blood' when a corpse is found, establishing communal responsibility, is in Deuteronomy 21:7 with the interpretation in Sotah 44b–45b that it means they did not send the victim off without food or escort. The teaching that one should not take leave of another except through words of Torah, because through this one remembers him, is stated in Berachot 31a. Jacob's seeing the wagons Joseph sent (Genesis 45:27) is read by Rashi on that verse as a sign Jacob understood through the last topic of Torah he had studied with Joseph — the eglah arufah (Deuteronomy 21) — where the word 'agalot' (wagons) recalls 'eglah' (heifer).", :sources ["Rashi on Genesis 42:2" "Seder Olam" "Deuteronomy 21:7" "Sotah 44b" "Berachot 31a" "Rashi on Genesis 45:27"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "עמק", :a-value 210, :b "רד\"ו", :b-value 210, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "וליוהו עד חברון", :spells "אבא חזור", :letters ["ו" "ד" "ן"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:10:32.928911263Z", :model "glm-5.2"}